'Abbott Names Texas Crime-Victims, Utility-Safety and Judiciary Appointees',
'The June 26, 2026, roundup covers seven crime-victims council reappointments, John Mason, David Evans and JJ Isbell; the missing June 28 article is reconstructed from records.',
NULL::text,'Keep TX Red Newsroom','Office of the Texas Governor','https://gov.texas.gov/news/post/governor-abbott-announces-latest-slate-of-appointments----____',
'2026-06-28T18:00:00Z'::timestamptz,'news','{"updated":"2026-10-14T19:42:00Z","intro":["Governor Greg Abbott released a summary of Texas board and commission appointments on June 26, 2026. The weekly roundup included seven reappointments to the Crime Victims'' Institute Advisory Council, John Mason''s appointment to the One-Call Board, David L. Evans'' reappointment as presiding judge of the Eighth Administrative Judicial Region, and JJ Isbell''s appointment to the State Commission on Judicial Conduct.","These were separate actions involving different state institutions and term lengths. The original June 28 Keep TX Red article describing the roundup was lost from the surviving newsroom records. This replacement preserves the historical address, checks each appointment against the governor''s June 26 report, and explains the public functions involved rather than making unsupported claims about the appointees'' subsequent actions."],"sections":[{"heading":"Seven members reappointed to crime-victims council","paragraphs":["The June 26 release said the governor reappointed Heather Ayala, Melissa Carter, Hillary England, Matthew L. Ferrara, Forrest Mitchell, Jeffery “JD” Robertson and David Schwartz to the Crime Victims'' Institute Advisory Council. Their terms were scheduled to expire January 31, 2028. This announcement was a slate of reappointments, not seven newly created council positions.","The governor described the advisory council as conducting research and analysis into the effects of crime on victims, survivors'' families, guardians and society. Its purpose connects policy and victim experiences with research rather than serving as a trial court. A reappointment record establishes membership terms, but not whether the council adopted a particular recommendation or produced a new study afterward."]},{"heading":"John Mason and excavation safety","paragraphs":["Abbott also appointed John Mason of Dallas to the One-Call Board of Texas with an initial term ending August 31, 2026. The governor''s separate June 24 announcement identifies him as senior legal counsel at AT&T and a former managing attorney for the Public Utility Commission of Texas. That John Mason must not be mistaken for Sean Mason, whom Abbott appointed on June 29 to lead the Office of State-Federal Relations.","The One-Call Board supports the state''s excavation-notification process, commonly recognized as Texas 811 or ''Call Before You Dig.'' Notifications help utilities identify buried lines before excavation, reducing accidental damage and service interruptions. The original board term was short; a September 15 governor''s release later listed John Mason among members reappointed through August 31, 2029."]},{"heading":"Presiding judge reappointed in Eighth Administrative Judicial Region","paragraphs":["The roundup identified David L. Evans as reappointed presiding judge of the Eighth Administrative Judicial Region, with the new term described as four years from qualification. Administrative judicial regions coordinate certain court-management responsibilities. The designation refers to regional judicial administration rather than a governor choosing the outcome of an individual lawsuit.","The governor''s summary provided the name, position and term but did not publish case-specific information about the courts Evans would manage or claim the judicial system had changed its rules because of the reappointment. Readers researching an individual judge''s rulings, courtroom assignments or complaint records should consult appropriate judicial sources; an appointment summary is not evidence of a decision in any particular proceeding."]},{"heading":"JJ Isbell selected for judicial conduct oversight","paragraphs":["The fourth category concerned the State Commission on Judicial Conduct. Abbott appointed JJ Isbell for a term scheduled to end December 31, 2027. The commission is responsible for examining judicial conduct and has formal powers relating to sanctions and recommendations concerning members of the judiciary under state law.","An appointment to that commission should not be confused with appointment to a judgeship, and it does not establish that any judge had been found guilty of misconduct. A separate governor''s announcement on July 1 named Martin “Ringo” Deleon Jr. of Corpus Christi to the same commission. Those distinct dates and names matter when reconstructing the historical sequence."]},{"heading":"Why a weekly appointments list matters to Texans","paragraphs":["State advisory councils, administrative boards and oversight commissions do not usually attract as much attention as statewide elections, but they carry defined responsibilities. An excavation safety board affects notification systems used by contractors and homeowners; a victims'' research council influences an evidence base for policy; a judicial administrative role shapes court operations; and a conduct commission receives and reviews formal complaints.","People seeking accountability need to know who was appointed, the legal scope of the institution, and the announced expiration of each term. The existence of a press release is not proof of subsequent attendance, voting, sanctions, regulatory changes or measurable program outcomes. Those require separate documents and context, especially when the appointment covered only a short portion of the year."]},{"heading":"Checking subsequent changes and the original record","paragraphs":["After June 26, Texas officials could fill new vacancies or extend members'' terms through additional appointments. For example, the governor''s September announcement concerning the One-Call Board superseded John Mason''s original short term. By contrast, the June release described the crime-victims advisory terms through January 2028 and the judicial conduct term through December 2027; this historical page does not assume newer changes absent documented updates.","The original Keep TX Red June 28 body has not survived. This article was reconstructed October 14, 2026, drawing directly from the governor''s June 26 roundup, the June 24 John Mason announcement, the July 1 Deleon notice and the board''s public information. It does not reproduce lost reporting as though recovered, and it avoids using a generic photograph as a representation of several unrelated appointment ceremonies."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What did Governor Abbott announce on June 26, 2026?","a":"A roundup of reappointments to the Crime Victims'' Institute Advisory Council, an One-Call Board appointment, an administrative judge reappointment and a judicial conduct commission appointment."},{"q":"Was Sean Mason appointed to the One-Call Board?","a":"No. The appointee was John Mason. Sean Mason was named separately to an office handling state–federal relations on June 29."},{"q":"Who was appointed to the State Commission on Judicial Conduct in the roundup?","a":"The June 26 summary named JJ Isbell; a separate July 1 statement named Martin “Ringo” Deleon Jr."}],"sources":[{"label":"Texas Governor — June 26 roundup of appointments","url":"https://gov.texas.gov/news/post/governor-abbott-announces-latest-slate-of-appointments----____"},{"label":"Texas Governor — June 24 John Mason appointment","url":"https://gov.texas.gov/news/post/governor-abbott-appoints-mason-to-one-call-board-of-texas"},{"label":"Texas Governor — July 1 Deleon appointment","url":"https://gov.texas.gov/news/post/governor-abbott-appoints-deleon-to-state-commission-on-judicial-conduct"},{"label":"Texas Governor — September 15 One-Call Board terms","url":"https://gov.texas.gov/news/post/governor-abbott-appoints-five-to-one-call-board-of-texas"},{"label":"One-Call Board of Texas — excavation notification duties","url":"https://onecalltexas.com/how-it-works/"}],"keyTakeaways":["The June 26 roundup documented four groups of personnel actions covering victims'' policy, excavation notification, court administration and judicial discipline.","John Mason of the One-Call Board is a different person from Sean Mason of the Office of State-Federal Relations.","The article transparently rebuilds a historical June 28 URL from official records rather than claiming recovery of the original text."]}'::jsonb,
